WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:08.450
Dr. Tabak: Arbor Day is a tree planting holiday
celebrated nationwide in our country in 1872

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:18.180
and April 25th is also DNA day when Watson,
Crick, Wilson and Franklin and colleagues

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:24.990
published their findings on the structure
of DNA in 1953.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:33.579
So today we can combine those celebrations
by planting a clone of the original tree of

00:00:33.579 --> 00:00:41.670
Hippocrates and by unveiling for the first
time the gene sequence for the tree.

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:46.680
We are in essence through science returning
the tree of Hippocrates to our campus and

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:54.210
also renewing, reinvigorating, and preserving
the historic living monument for prosperity.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:01:01.850
Dr. Lindberg: The tree of course was given
by the ambassador of Greece in '61, planted

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:09.490
in '62 and our institution began business
so to speak in 1962 so the National Library

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.390
of Medicine and the tree grew up together.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:17.079
Dr. Constantine Stratakis: Under this famous
tree in Kos, in the island of Kos where he

00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:24.850
mentored practitioners of his art throughout
the known world at the time people of all

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:30.570
religions, backgrounds and ethnicities this
was the NIH of the 5th century.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:36.670
His Excellency Christos P. Panagopoulos: Hippocrates
used to gather his disciples around him and

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:45.329
the shadow of a plane tree and this tree lives
in this great institution today.

00:01:45.329 --> 00:01:52.219
It took American ingenuity and dedication
to clone this in a way as I understand it

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:55.940
and have this dedication again today.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:01.960
Dr. Lipman: The DNA barcode of life project,
it is a fascinating project for hundreds and

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:08.250
hundreds of thousands of organisms, the goal
is to have a physical specimen from that organism

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:14.830
available in a museum for study and along
with that a short DNA sequence from that organism

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.750
and this is very important for conservation
efforts and for basic research.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:26.580
Dr. Amy Driscoll from the Smithsonian National
Museum of Natural History downtown took a

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:34.890
tiny DNA sample from the original clone that
we had from 50 years ago and had that sequenced

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:42.110
so now we know where the Tree of Hippocrates
is standing in the overall tree of life.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:47.610
And I think he would've been very excited
about what DNA comparison and other how modern

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:53.360
methods are being used to treat disease.

